  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">SAFEGUARDING ELEPHANT POPULATIONS IN SOUTHERN KENYA THROUGH COEXISTENCE AND CONNECTIVITY</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">THE PURPOSE OF THIS PROJECT IS TO SUPPORT AN ELEPHANT CONSERVATION EFFORT ON COMMUNITY-OWNED LAND BETWEEN TSAVO, CHYULU HILLS, AND AMBOSELI NATIONAL PARKS, AND MAASAI MARA RESERVE BY STRENGTHENING ELEPHANT PROTECTION AND COMMUNITY ENGAGEMENT, AND BUILDING A CONSTITUENCY FOR ELEPHANT CONSERVATION. AS PART OF THE KENYA RIFT VALLEY SAVANNA ELEPHANT STRONGHOLD, THIS ECOSYSTEM IS HOME TO APPROXIMATELY 12,814 ELEPHANTS. THIS PROJECT IS INTENDED TO CONSERVE AFRICAN SAVANNA ELEPHANTS AND THEIR HABITAT BY ADDRESSING THE IMPACTS OF POACHING AND HUMAN-ELEPHANT CONFLICT (HEC). SPECIFIC ACTIVITIES INCLUDE: (1) FACILITATING PEER-TO-PEER CONSERVATION LEARNING AND EXCHANGE AMONG COMMUNITY AND OPINION LEADERS (2) CREATING OPPORTUNITIES FOR CONSERVATION-RELATED INVESTMENT IN UNDERFUNDED REGIONS, INCLUDING IDENTIFICATION OF POTENTIAL TOURISM SITES AND DEVELOPING PROPOSALS (3) EXPANDING YOUTH CONSERVATION LEADERSHIP PROGRAMMING TO SCHOOLS LOCATED IN KEY ELEPHANT CORRIDORS (4) PROVIDING OPERATIONAL SUPPORT AND COMMUNITY-BASED NATURAL RESOURCE MANAGEMENT (CBNRM) TRAINING TO COMMUNITY RANGERS AND (5) CONDUCTING HEC MITIGATION AND RAPID RESPONSE INTERVENTIONS.</narrative>
  </description>
